  ],
  "text": "Zero is not obvious. Some of the greatest mathematicians in history worked entirely without it. Try multiplying one thousand seven hundred and seventy six by forty two in Roman numerals. You cannot. There is nowhere to carry into, because there are no columns. Roman numerals are for recording a number, not for working with one. The fix is a symbol that means this column is empty. Once you have that, position carries meaning, and arithmetic becomes something you can do on paper. But a placeholder is not yet a number you can do anything with. In seventh century India, Brahmagupta wrote the rules for treating it as a quantity. It travelled through the Arab world into Europe, where several cities banned it as suspicious. Nothing had to be invented. Then it had to be argued for. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
